artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a solar flare modeled as a magnetized plasma system in which magnetic reconnection releases stored magnetic energy and produces nonthermal populations of energetic electrons (with energies in the deka- to hecto-keV range) and energetic ions (with energies ranging from about 0.01 to 100 MeV). Several candidate acceleration mechanisms have been proposed:

(a) merging of magnetic islands/plasmoids inside reconnecting current sheets;
(b) acceleration at fast-mode termination shocks formed where reconnection outflows impinge on closed flare loops;
(c) direct (DC) parallel electric fields in the reconnection region;
(d) stochastic/turbulent acceleration, including acceleration by turbulence generated within fragmented current sheets;
(e) local acceleration in the chromosphere.

Address the following questions:

1. Identify the dominant mechanism(s) in the list above responsible for accelerating electrons and ions in solar flares, and determine the spatial location(s) within the flare geometry where the bulk of the acceleration occurs.
2. Quantify how the relative contributions of these mechanisms vary from flare to flare and within the temporal evolution of a single flare.
3. Determine which physical parameters of a flare control its efficiency as a particle accelerator, measured both by the total number of accelerated particles and by the maximum energy attained in the resulting distributions.
4. Determine the pitch-angle anisotropy of the accelerated electron and ion distributions.
5. Determine whether electrons and protons are accelerated by a common process (co-spatial and co-temporal) or by separate mechanisms, and characterize the relationship between their spectra, numbers, and timing.
6. Determine the ratio of the total energy carried by energetic ions to that carried by energetic electrons, and the partition of energy between these populations as a function of flare properties.
